{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/kernel at bed3662615a120b3ef4d79aaeac57de5d28afa24: the sequencer's overload refusal, rebuilt on fresh authorization.\n\nOver a configured MaxQueueDepth, Submitter.Submit returns ErrBackPressure after one atomic add taken before the lock. A refused submission therefore parses no intent, never reaches the pre-append hook, writes no payload tree, and never chains — the refusal precedes all of it rather than following the expensive part. Zero remains unbounded, which is the embedding opt-out for a kernel used outside this application.\n\nWHAT CHANGED SINCE THE REJECTED HEAD. Rotation no longer reports back-pressure. Its exhausted retry budget returns the anonymous result it always had. I had classified it as capacity and proved it with a negative retry count, which runs the loop zero times and so demonstrates that an invalid configuration returns the sentinel, not that exhaustion does. The honest alternative was a production failpoint inside Rotate existing only so a test could reach the line, and a seam that exists for tests is worse than the classification it would prove. The sentinel's comment now says plainly that rotation keeps its own result and why.\n\nTESTS, each checked against the mutation it exists for rather than accepted green.\n- Capacity: the refusal is prompt as well as correct. Moving the gate after the lock — where it still refuses, but only after queuing — fails the test. That is what separates \"before chaining\" from \"returns an error\".\n- The first arrival is now bounded. Previously a wrongly refused first slot would have hung until the ten-minute test timeout instead of reporting the refusal that caused it.\n- The unbounded case queues four followers, not one. One follower shows only that nothing was refused, which a limit of two would also satisfy.\n\nQueueDepth is new and small: it reports the bound this submitter was built with, so a deployment can prove its own posture instead of trusting that the option was passed. It exists because the previous head shipped an option nobody set; the app-side test that catches that regression needs to read the constructed object, and package app cannot read an unexported kernel field. I would rather a reviewer judged that addition than have it arrive unexplained.\n\nGates at this exact head are reported in the review request; the resident-side change and the limits documentation are separate artifacts at internal/app and docs.\n\nThis rests on the authorizing request, the promise, and d97eed89, the live artifact for what main carries at this path. My earlier candidate fda8bc9b describes head f116097a, which was changes-requested and will not merge; it is retired separately naming this successor.","body":{"commit":"bed3662615a120b3ef4d79aaeac57de5d28afa24","path":"internal/kernel"}}